    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d407">

                <pb facs="0487" n="389" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg389"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.428/623</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the British Chargé</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Craigie</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-02-02"><hi rendition="#italic">February 2,
                        1921</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of the Ambassador’s note No. 837 of December 28, 1920, inquiring, at the
                    instance of the Government of Canada, what prospect there is of the Treaty
                    signed on May 25th between the United States and Great Britain for the
                    protection, preservation and propagation of salmon fisheries in <pb facs="0488"
                        n="390" xml:id="pg390"/> the waters contiguous to Canada and the United
                    States, receiving action by the Senate during the present session of Congress.
                    In reply I regret to say that I have ascertained that by reason of the desire of
                    persons interested in the Pacific coast fisheries to have an opportunity to be
                    heard regarding the Treaty, there is little probability of action being taken on
                    it by the Committee on Foreign Relations during the present session.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Norman
                            H. Davis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
